{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_fyvg5k5oxwq6hk6iuvyqr26yeph4wmm3lvfuzurgnxinpwpcoj5q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. Zero warnings, zero errors, empty symbol diff, all 6 configurations green.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src` is a single translation unit exporting three symbols: `cleanup(int,int,int,int)`, `print_result(const char*,int)`, `cleanup_resources(char*)`. Its behavior is dominated by three quirks the translation had to preserve exactly: a `switch` with deliberate fall-through (`case 10` → `+30`, `case 30` → `+70`), `TO_STRING(numbers)` stringizing the *macro argument* (printing the literal text `numbers`, not the array), and two error branches that both `goto cleanup` and return `result`, so only stdout distinguishes success from failure.\n\n## Artifacts created (all in `translation/`)\n\n| file | contents |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` | 3 C exports, all present in Rust; `ldd -r` clean |\n| `ERRORS.md` | 15 rows, mechanically derived — all `[x]` |\n| `CONFIGS.md` | 28 rows across 9 shape axes — all `[x]` |\n| `VERIFICATION.md` | completion gate + reproduction steps |\n| `tests/` | 48 differential tests, both `.so`s loaded via `libloading` |\n\n## The one real bug found and fixed\n\n`src/lib.rs` folded away its `strncmp` call in release: LLVM proved both operands were the same constant, so the release `.so` did not import `strncmp` at all while the C `.so` always calls it. Routing the pointers through `core::hint::black_box` restores the observable libc call sequence. Everything else matched the C on the first run.\n\n## Two things worth flagging about the method\n\n**libtest had to go.** The tests redirect fd 1 to capture the exact bytes each library writes through C stdio — but libtest writes its own progress to fd 1, and it was being interleaved into the captured output (visible as `Rust= \"Processed numbers: numbers\\ntest row06_all_permutations...\"`). The test targets now use `harness = false` with a sequential runner that reports on stderr.\n\n**Two error branches are unreachable through the public API**, so happy-path testing cannot touch them: the `strncmp(\"VALID\",\"VALID\")` check can never fail, and `malloc(50)` never fails in practice. I compiled an `LD_PRELOAD` shim (`tests/support/fault_shim.c`) that interposes `malloc`/`free`/`strncmp` ahead of libc — reaching *both* `.so`s identically, which is what keeps it a fair differential test — and armed it only around each individual FFI call. That same shim closed a blind spot: `free` returns nothing and prints nothing, so a translation that simply *never freed* would have passed every output comparison. Allocation bookkeeping now asserts 400 allocations ↔ 400 frees, correct pointer identity, and zero frees on both failure paths.\n\nI validated the suite is not vacuous with three negative controls — removing the fall-through, removing the `black_box`, and replacing `free` with a no-op — each caught by the expected tests, in both profiles. One caveat on scope: `cleanup_resources` on a non-heap or already-freed pointer, and unterminated label buffers, are excluded as C-level UB with no defined behavior to match; both exclusions are documented in `ERRORS.md`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":401,"cache_read_input_tokens":145731,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":401,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"83900659-f63e-4ca6-9ca7-35069ca6af51","uuid":"5f345a9f-8dd8-4935-9646-ea20419a221b","timestamp":"2026-08-27T23:44:27.628Z"}
